//BUG="copying one void variable into another emits two `void` locals, which gcc rejects"
//OUT="ok"

// `s = r` has a void variable on both sides. Neither has storage once void
// variables are erased and the right-hand side is not a call, so the whole
// statement is a no-op and should disappear. It is the one shape where erasing
// the assignment as well as the declaration is correct, which is why it is
// worth having next to 28_a_bound_void_call_still_runs.cpl.

fn nothing
    ret

fn main
    r = nothing()
    s = r
    out "ok"
